; Cranelisp under the hood — the allocation ledger, and who releases your result

; This demo's subject is the runtime, not a language feature. It uses /mem to
; watch heap values being reclaimed. The invariant on display: a value you type
; at the prompt is displayed IN FULL and then released EXACTLY ONCE, so a
; session that keeps producing heap values does not keep growing.

; /mem with no argument is a snapshot of the runtime's counters. `live` is the
; part that matters: allocations that have been made and not yet freed.
/mem

; A prelude-loading session starts with a nonzero `live` — loading the stdlib
; expands macros, and the macro-expansion boundary retains its marshalled
; arguments by design. That baseline is a constant, not a growth rate: it is
; the subject of its own known defect, and this demo reads it as the FLOOR
; that every measurement below sits on.

; A type with one heap field, so each value is exactly two allocations:
; the string, and the box that holds it.
(deftype Box [:String contents])

; Now the experiment. Note `live` here, then produce the same heap value
; several times, then read `live` again.
/mem

(Box "boxed")

(Box "boxed")

(Box "boxed")

; `allocs` and `deallocs` each advanced by six — two per value, three values.
; `live` did not move at all. Each result was formatted first, then released.
/mem

; The same holds for a value with structure. A three-node tree is five
; allocations; produce two of them and `live` still does not move.
(deftype Tree (Leaf [:Int v]) (Node [:Tree l :Tree r]))

(Node (Leaf 1) (Node (Leaf 2) (Leaf 3)))

(Node (Leaf 1) (Node (Leaf 2) (Leaf 3)))

; Ten more allocations, ten more deallocations, `live` unchanged — the release
; walks the whole value, not just its outermost box.
/mem

; And for a library type over a heap payload:
(Some "heap payload")

(Some "heap payload")

/mem

; Order matters, and it is the same order in every mode. The REPL renders the
; whole `:Type value` line before releasing; --run computes its exit code
; before releasing; a linked binary converts before releasing. You never see a
; half-formatted value, and nothing is released twice.

; The displayed type does not decide the release. Below, the same value is
; produced twice with a residual type parameter left in its type, then twice
; under an annotation that pins it.
/mem

(Err "boom")

(Err "boom")

; `allocs` and `deallocs` each advanced by four — two per value — and `live`
; did not move. The unpinned `a` in the displayed type changes nothing.
/mem

; Pin the parameter and the ledger reads the same:
:(Result String String) (Err "boom")

:(Result String String) (Err "boom")

; Four and four again, `live` flat. The two displays differ; the release does
; not.
/mem

; A closing note on the tool itself. `/mem <expr>` reports a per-expression
; delta instead of a snapshot. It displays the result, releases it, and only
; then closes its measurement window, so the same heap value reads `live +0`
; here too: one line answers "did this expression's memory get reclaimed?"
/mem (Box "boxed")

; [end]
